{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"Wire the copilot, codex, and claude client configs to connect to the rebar MCP endpoint over remote\nHTTP with a `static` bearer PAT (ADR deft-evolutive-mosasaur; PATs from exuberant-blockish-avians).\nSingle concern: the three client-side config shapes + a basic read-tool smoke — the endpoint/edge is\nesok, the credentials are avians, live prod verification is jackal.\n\n## Approach + rationale (research-confirmed static bearer for all three)\nResearch confirmed all three clients support a static `Authorization: Bearer <PAT>` header for a\nremote HTTP MCP server and none require OAuth, so one auth shape covers all three:\n- **Copilot**: `~/.copilot/mcp-config.json` — the rebar server entry (`type: http`) with the HTTPS\n  `/mcp/` URL and a `headers` map carrying `Authorization: Bearer $MCP_CLIENT_PAT_COPILOT` (the CLI\n  expands `$VAR` from the environment, so no PAT literal is stored).\n- **Codex**: `~/.codex/config.toml` — a `[mcp_servers.rebar]` streamable-HTTP entry using\n  `bearer_token_env_var = \"MCP_CLIENT_PAT_CODEX\"` so the PAT comes from the environment, not the\n  committed file.\n- **Claude**: `.mcp.json` (`type: http`, `headers` with `Authorization: Bearer ${MCP_CLIENT_PAT_CLAUDE}`)\n  or `claude mcp add --transport http --header`. Document the gotcha: a static header takes\n  precedence and does NOT fall back to OAuth if rejected, so a wrong/expired PAT fails hard (not a\n  silent OAuth retry).\nEach references the gitignored credential from avians (env var / gitignored `mcp-clients.local.json`),\nnever an inline secret. Ship copy-ready example configs under `examples/mcp-clients/` plus a setup\ndoc; the three client-home files themselves are not committed.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Tier: **real client config load + a basic read-tool call** (test-design §3 \"external-service\n  mapping\" via a local `rebar-mcp` HTTP instance the test spins up on loopback with a known static\n  token). Oracles:\n  - Each of the three committed example configs is accepted by its client and lists the `rebar` MCP\n    server (assert via each client's server-list command: `copilot mcp get rebar`,\n    `codex mcp list`, `claude mcp list`).\n  - A basic read tool (a ticket `show`/`list`) executes successfully through the HTTP transport with\n    the bearer header resolved from each config (oracle: a real tool result payload, not just\n    \"connected\").\n  - Contrast (§6): an absent/invalid bearer yields a 401 auth failure surfaced to the client, not a\n    hang — confirming the header is actually on the request path.\n- [ ] No secret is committed (the PAT is sourced from the avians env var / gitignored file); a\n  test/grep asserts no PAT literal appears in the committed example configs.\n\n## Testing / Verification\n- Proof command: `make test PYTEST_ARGS=tests/interfaces/facades/test_mcp_client_configs.py`\n  (the new hermetic test spins up an in-process/loopback `rebar-mcp` HTTP server with a static\n  token, drives a real read-tool call through each resolved client config, asserts the 401 contrast,\n  and greps the committed example configs for any PAT literal).\n- Full gate: `make lint && make typecheck && make test`.\n\n## Compat / rollback\nAdditive client config; rollback = remove the three server entries / the `examples/mcp-clients/`\nfiles. No server-side change.\n\nDepends on: cibophobic-holohedral-esok (reachable endpoint), exuberant-blockish-avians (PATs).\nBlocks: creased-childlike-jackal (live prod verification)."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1787623546070914001,"uuid":"6085a8f1-c7a8-4280-a9bb-f696b5cffdcf"}